### Test Case 1: Updating Entries
This test checks if updates are correctly handled when merging two existing records into a single record.

#### Step-by-step Explanation:
1. **Original Data**: We start by creating three `DRMerge` objects representing different sets of updated records (`mem1`, `mem2`, and `mem3`) and their respective keys and corresponding `drm` values.

   ```java
   DrumStoreContext drcm = new DrumStore();
   DRMerge mem1, mems, memv2;
   ```

2. **Writing Records**:
   - We call `dataStore.writeEntries()` to write all three merged records to the cache database.

     ```java  
       Set<Long> keys = new LinkedHashSet<>(Arrays.stream(drcmd.getEntries()).mapToLong(DRMEdit::getKeyId).toArray(Set<Long>::new));
       Set<Integer> vals = new TreeSet<>();

       for (long k : keys) {
         vals.addAll(Arrays.stream(drcread.getValues(k)).boxed().collect(Collectors.toList()));
       } 
       for(int i=0;i<vals.size();i++) {
          drcread.put(key, Arrays.toString(vals.subList(i,i+1)));     
       }

       dataStore.updateMem(mem1.getKey(), mem1.getVal());      
       data_store.updateMem(mems.getKey(), memes.getVal());
       data.storeEntries(keys,vals);    
     ```

   This step involves writing multiple entries to the database simultaneously, which can lead to performance issues due to race conditions. However, it simulates real-world scenarios where such large quantities might need careful handling.

